## 1. Introduction
\(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is the most important part of the greenhouse gas with the ratio of \(63\%\) , and it mainly comes from the burning of fossil fuels [1]. Although the development of clean energy is greatly improved in recent years, the fossil fuels still occupy the main position of the present energy. Therefore, it is important to develop the effective and feasible strategies to reduce the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration after the burning of the fossil fuels. On the other hand, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is a kind of rich C1 resource, and converting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into the high-value products such as CO, \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) , and methanol is a research hotspot in chemical industry, environment and energy, and hence attracts increasing attention in recent years [2,3]. As compared with the traditional energy sources, solar energy has the unique advantages such as large reserve and low pollution, and hence using solar energy to convert \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into the high-value products can not only alleviate the problem of energy shortage, but also reduce the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration [4]. However, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is a very stable molecule with a high \(\scriptstyle{C=0}\) dissociation energy, which makes its photocatalytic reduction a great challenge.
