n the pores and provides advantageous condition for its further photocatalytic reduction. The abundant unsaturated metal sites of the MOFs can provide more catalytic sites [8]. Noticeably, the tunable light collection and efficient electron-hole (e-h) separation capabilities of the MOFs in the visible range give them great potential for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction [9,10]. However, the weak coordination between the metal ions and organic ligands makes the MOFs unstable, which seriously restricts their practical application [3]. On the other hand, the more recently developed covalent-organic frameworks (COFs), which are prepared by the reversible condensation reaction under thermodynamic control, are a kind of emerging porous crystalline materials [11]. As compared with the MOFs, the COFs possess highly physicochemical stability, and they are extensively applied in the fields of adsorption [12], energy storage [13], and catalysis [14,15]. In addition, the COFs have highly long-range ordered \(\pi\)-conjugated system, which can effectively accelerate the electron transfer [16], and hence they are also employed for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction [17]. Nevertheless, the rapid recombination of the photogenerated e-h pairs reduces their photocatalytic efficiency [18].If the MOFs were used as the substrate in the synthetic process of the COFs, and the COFs will be in-situ coated on the surface of the MOFs. The as-prepared MOFs@COFs hybrid materials not only foster the strengths and circumvent weaknesses, and their performance may be greatly improved due to their structural synergistic effect [19]. For example, Gao et al. [20] prepared a core–shell PCN-222-Co@TpPa-1 hybrid material through \(\pi\)-\(\pi\) stacking interaction. It is found that it can catalyze the cascade deacetalization Knoevenagel condensation reaction in one step, with the conversion rate up to \(99.3\%\). More recently, Peng et al. [21] coated TPA-COFs on the surface