{2}\)-MIL-125 in this process. As expected, the obtained hybrid materials had higher physicochemical stability due to the formation of the \(\scriptstyle{C=N}\) covalent bonds between the core and the shell. Moreover, the typical type-II heterojunction structure was successfully generated in their interface, which acted as the bridges to accelerate the charge transfer and enhanced the separation efficiency of photogenerated electrons and holes. And the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic ability of the hybrid materials exhibited stronger than the single component. Our work can provide helpful ideas for the careful design of the novel MOFs@COFs hybrid materials as well as useful exploration for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction.
