## 2.3. Synthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125, COF-OH and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@ COF-OH
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 was synthesized by a solvothermal method according to reference [23]. COF-OH was synthesized by room temperature method. \(24.92\ \mathrm{mg}\ 2,5\)-Dihydroxyterephthalaldehyde, \(35.45\ \mathrm{mg}\ 4,4',4''\)-(1,3,5-Triazine-2,4,6-triyl)trianiline (TAPP) and \(5.0~\mathrm{mL}\) acetonitrile were added in a Pyrex pressure tube and sonicated about \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) until the solution was cleared. And then \(0.4\;\mathrm{mL}\) \(12\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid was added dropwise, which the precipitation was appeared. And then frozen under liquid \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) bath and degassed by three freeze–pumpthaw cycles. Thereafter, the tube was sealed off and left undisturbed at \(30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 days. A yellow precipitate was gained by filtration, washed with DCM, THF, acetone, and methanol. The product was further purified in a Soxhlet extractor with THF and methanol for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Then the product was collected after drying under vacuum at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) .
The synthesis method of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@COF-OH was similar to that of COF-OH. \(9.97\ \mathrm{mg}\ 2,5\)-dihydroxyterephthalaldehyde was added in \(3.0~\mathrm{mL}\) acetonitrile and sonicated dissolves completely, \(10\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 was added and continue to sonicated about
20 min to make the mixture into a uniform suspension. Then added \(2.0\,\mathrm{mL}\) acetonitrile solution containing \(14.18\,\mathrm{mg}\) TAPP to the above suspension and continues sonication for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\). \(0.4\,\mathrm{mL}\) \(12\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid was added dropwise and then frozen under liquid \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) bath and degassed by three freeze–pumpthaw cycles. Thereafter, the tube was sealed off and left undisturbed at \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 days. Finally the product was filtration, washed, and Soxhlet extractor with organic solvent. Then the product was collected after drying under vacuum at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), named \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@COF-OH-1. The \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@COF-OHs with different shell thickness was prepared by changing the different addition amounts of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125. When the amounts of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 were \(20\,\mathrm{mg}\), \(30\,\mathrm{mg}\), and \(40\,\mathrm{mg}\), the prepared \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@COF-OHs were named \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@COF-OH-2, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@COF-OH-3 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125@COF-OH-4, respectively.
## 2.4. Photoelectric performance test
The electrochemical tests were performed by CHI660e workstation. A standard three-electrode system was used, with \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) as the reference electrode, Pt sheet as the counter electrode, and the sample coated on FTO glass as the working electrode. The preparation method of the working electrode was as follows: Dispersed \(5\,\mathrm{mg}\) of the sample in \(475\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ethanol, sonicate for \(40\,\mathrm{min}\) to make it evenly dispersed, and then added \(25\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of Nafion membrane solution to continue sonication for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\). Then, \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the obtained solution was dripped onto the FTO glass and dried. The light source used in the test is a Xe light source (PLS-SXE300). Electrochemical workstation can be used to measure photocurrent response, electrochemical impedance and Mott-Schottky curve.
## 2.5. Photocatalytic performance test
The performance test of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction was carried out in a reactor with a volume of \(100\,\mathrm{mL}\), and the process used a PLS-SXE300 Xe lamp light source with \(300\,\mathrm{W}\) to simulate the natural light. The specific operation steps were as follows: Disperse \(10\,\mathrm{mg}\) of the sample in amount of deionized water and sonicated to make it evenly mixed. Then transfer it to a small evaporating dish and vacuum dried to remove moisture, finally obtain a catalyst film. \(0.084\,\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}\) was weighted and placed at the bottom of the reactor, and the prepared sample was placed in it. The reactor was evacuated, then the Ar gas was pushed in reactor about \(20\,\mathrm{min}\), and then \(0.3\,\mathrm{mL}\) of \(2\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) was slowly injected into the reactor to fully react with \(\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}\) to generate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Finally, turn on the Xe lamp to illuminate the reactor, the distance between the lamp and the sample was about \(20\,\mathrm{cm}\). The gas product was carried out samples per \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) and then detected by GC. Each data was measured three times in parallel.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structural characterization
The morphology of the samples was characterized by SEM and Fig. 1(a, f) indicated that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 had a regular smooth cake-like morphology with the thickness of about \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) and the particle size of about \(700\,\mathrm{nm}\). After growing COF-OH (Fig. S1) on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125, it is clear from Fig. 1(b-e, g-j) that the COF-OH was evenly coated on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125, and the resulting samples presented much rougher surface. The thinner the shell is, the rougher the surface is. The EDS in Fig. 1(k-o) displayed the uniform distribution of C, N, O, and Ti elements, and the TEM images (Fig. 1(p-t) and Fig. S2) demonstrated that COF-OH was uniformly covered on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125, and the favorable core–shell structure was formed through the heterogeneous nucleation. The shell thickness was precisely controlled by adjusting the feeding amount of the MOFs, implying the controllable synthesis of the hybrid materials.
OH was uniformly covered on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125, and the favorable core–shell structure was formed through the heterogeneous nucleation. The shell thickness was precisely controlled by adjusting the feeding amount of the MOFs, implying the controllable synthesis of the hybrid materials.
The \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 core and COF-OH shell was proposed to be covalently connected by the stable \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) bonds and the FT-IR and the XPS spectra confirmed this conclusion. After coating the COF-OH shell on the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 core according to the Schiff-based reaction, Fig. 2a and Fig. S3 displayed the characteristic \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) stretching at \(1650\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [24]. As compared with the original \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125, the hybrid material had another \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) configuration in the high-resolution C-1-3 (Fig. 2b). Additionally, the high-resolution N 1 s of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 in Fig. 2c exhibited the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) configuration at \(402.58\,\mathrm{eV}\) [25]. After their combination, this configuration obviously disappeared, while another \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) configuration presented at \(398.08\,\mathrm{eV}\), implying the successful \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) covalent connection between the core and the shell. The phase formation of the samples was verified by PXRD patterns and it can be concluded from Fig. 2d that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 and COF-OH maintained their integrity in the resulting hybrid materials, and few changes occurred for the diffraction peaks with different shell thickness (Fig. S5).
The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms of the samples in Fig. 2e and Fig. S6 had the type-IV profile, indicative of abundant micropores and mesopores. According to the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms, the porous parameters were predicted and the results are summarized in Table 1. The \(S_{\mathrm{BET}}\) of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 and COF-OH were measured to be \(1239\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(824\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. The \(S_{\mathrm{BET}}\) of the prepared hybrid materials ranged \(451\!-\!930\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\!\cdot\!\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and it increased with increasing the feeding amount of the MOFs. At the same time, the as-prepared hybrid materials owned considerable \(S_{\mathrm{micro}}\), \(V_{\mathrm{total}}\) and \(V_{\mathrm{micro}}\). The PSD was calculated by the NLDFT model (Fig. 2f), and the pores of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125 were primarily concentrated at \(0.68\) and \(0.93\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively, while COF-OH had the pores mainly predominated at \(1.27\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(2.73\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. After their hybridization, the micropores at \(0.93\,\mathrm{nm}\) of the MOFs and those at \(1.27\,\mathrm{nm}\) of the COFs evidently disappeared, while another micropore at \(1.18\,\mathrm{nm}\) appeared, demonstrating that some new micropores were generated at the interfacial layer between the core and the shell. The present results implied that the special structure was formed between the MOFs and the COFs, which enhanced its \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture and photocatalytic reduction performance. In addition, the hybrid materials with different shell thickness had the similar PSD.
