The optical adsorption of the samples was investigated and Fig. S9 exhibited that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH-3 had a great redshifted absorption edge (at \(606~\mathrm{nm}\) ) as compared with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL125 (at \(495\;\mathrm{nm}\) ), which means that the synthesized hybrid materials had a broadened light absorption range and the formed \(\scriptstyle{C=\Nu}\) bonds between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 and COF-OH should play a role. After forming the stable heterojunction structure at the interface, the interaction between them was greatly strengthened [31]. According to the Kubelka-Munk function [32], the band gap energy \((E_{\mathrm{g}})\) of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH-3 was determined to be \(2.16\ \mathrm{\eV},\) between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 \((2.71\ \mathrm{eV})\) and COF-OH \((2.13\,\mathrm{\eV},\) Fig. 3d). Moreover, the \(E_{\mathrm{g}}\) was significantly decreased to \(2.16~\mathrm{eV}\) after the growth of the COFs on the surface of the MOFs, suggesting that the prepared hydride materials were more favorable for the photocatalytic reaction. In addition, the XPS valence band spectra (Fig. 3 (e, f)) revealed that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 and COF-OH had the valence band (VB) of \(2.03\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(0.78\,{\mathrm{eV}}_{\mathrm{;}}\) , respectively. Therefore, their conduction band (CB) can be calculated to be \(-0.68~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(-1.35\;\mathrm{eV},\) respectively.
## 3.3. \(C O_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction
The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms of the samples were measured with the temperature at \(273\mathrm{~K~}\) (Fig. 4a and Fig S11) and the pressure ranged from 0.02 bar to 1.0 bar, it is noticeable that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-125@COF-OH-3 had a relatively higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity \((0.18\;\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2})\) than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 \((0.17\;\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2})\) and much greater \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity than COF-OH \((0.07\mathrm{~mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-2})\) at 1.0 bar and \(273\,\mathrm{~K~}\) , implied that the particular interface between the MOFs and the COFs made a great positive contribution. The synergistic effect was present between the core and the shell, which made the resulting hybrid materials perform better for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration in the pores was greatly increased after the adsorption, which can accelerate the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction [22]. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) -TPD was used to determine the basicity of catalyst surfaces [33,34] and the results were collected in Fig. 4b. By comparing with single \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 and COF-OH, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL\(125@C O F-O H-3\) had a stronger Lewis base property, which was more favorable for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption and conversion.
Thereafter, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic performance was carried out in the gas–solid reaction under the atmosphere of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . It is seen that the CO yield of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125, COF-OH, and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL\(125@C O F-O H\) gradually increased with increasing the catalysis
time, and the hydride materials owned a higher CO yield than the single components (Fig. S11). Among these samples, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH-3 exhibited the highest CO yield of \(22.93\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.\) - \(\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) , about 2 times higher than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 \((11.82\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) and 3 times higher than COF-OH \((7.26\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) (Fig. 4c). The excellent properties exhibited by the hybrid materials may be attributed to the formed stable \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) covalent bonds could facilitate the e-h pairs separation at the interface, and the photogenerated electrons were efficiently transferred from \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 to COFOH through the \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) bonds. The synergism of the MOFs and the COFs, i.e., extended light absorption, efficient exciton dissolution and transfer, and the appropriate porous parameters gave positive contributions. Particularly, the new generated pores with the pore size of \(1.18\ \mathrm{nm}\) at the interface may serve as the nanoreactors to confine the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) catalytic reaction and thus facilitate the mass and electron transfer [35]. It had the comparable CO yield with the reported values of the MOFs- or COFs-based materials in the literature (Table S2). In this reaction system, when \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was replaced by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) , the CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) yield could be negligible, indicated that CO was generated from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . It can be further confirmed by \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) isotope tracer experiment and the signal peak at \(m/z=45\) belonged to \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . After photocatalysis, the MS signal peaks of CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) were generated at \(m/z=29\) and \(m/z=17\) , respectively (Fig. 4d). The reusability performance of the sample was carried
out and its CO yield remained at \(20.21\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) after 5 repeated cycles, demonstrating the good regeneration and repeated use performance (Fig. 4e). After coating COF-OH on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125, the thermal stability of the resulting materials was greatly improved (Fig. 4f, g2). Moreover, the SEM, FT-IR, PXRD, and XPS of the samples after the catalysis process were characterized, and Fig. S13 and Fig. S14 indicated that no evident changes happened, revealing its structural stability.
EPR spectra of DMPO- \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) was measured and the results were shown in Fig. 4f. It was found that the DMPO-OH signals of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-125 was stronger than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH-3, suggested that the photogenerated holes of hydride materials did not stay in the VB of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125. Therefore the electron transfer pathway should be following the conventional type II heterojunction. This result can be further verified by UPS and according to the UPS in Fig. S15 and Fig. 4g, the work functions (WF) and Fermi level of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 were scaled to be \(3.75\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(-0.69\,{\mathrm{eV}}\) , respectively, while those of COF-OH were \(4.10~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(-0.34~\mathrm{eV}\) , respectively. That is, the electrons flowed from \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 to COF-OH through the heterojunction structure until the Fermi level reached the equilibrium (Fig. 4h). Therefore, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 was expected to be positively charged while COF-OH was negatively charged, an internal electric field pointing from \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 to COF-OH was resultingly generated at the interface. As the electrons in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -
MIL-125 were repelled by the internal electric field, the potential energy increased, and the energy band bended upward [36]. On the contrary, the potential energy of COF-OH decreased, and the energy band bended downward. As a consequence, the type-II heterojunction was proven to be formed between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 and COF-OH, and the typical electron transfer process was described in Fig. 4i.
Subsequently, the possible \(C O_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction mechanism on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH-3 was proposed in Fig. 4j. After the UV–visible light irradiation, plentiful photogenerated electrons were constantly generated at the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-125 as well as COF-OH and transferred to their corresponding CB. On the contrary, the photogenerated holes still stayed at their corresponding VB [37,38]. The CB of COF-OH \((-1.35\mathrm{~eV})\) was more negative than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 \((-0.68\mathrm{~eV})\) , and thus the photogenerated electrons were transferred from the CB of COF-OH to \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-125 via the covalently connected \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) bonds. Consequently, they were heavily accumulated on the CB of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125. The CB of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 was more negative than \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}\) \((-0.53\,\mathrm{\eV})\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) \((-0.24\,\mathrm{eV})\) . As a result, \(C O_{2}\) was catalytically converted to CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) [37]. Correspondingly, the photogenerated holes were migrated from the VB of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 to COF-OH through the stable \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) covalent bonds at the interfacial layer, resulting in the oxidation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . The continuous migration of the photogenerated electrons and holes facilitated the e-h pairs separation, thereby improved the \(C O_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction performance.
Some other COFs, namely, COF-Br, SCOF-OH and SCOF-Br, were thereafter prepared by changing the monomers skeleton or the substituting groups. For example, using 2,5- dibromoterephthalaldehyde to replace 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalal dehyde, and it had the Schiff-based reaction with \(^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}\) -(1,3,5-tria zine-2,4,6-triyl) trianiline, and hence another COF-Br was synthesized. As 2,4,6-tris(4-aminophenyl) benzene was employed in place of \(^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}\) -(1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6-triyl) trianiline, the SCOF-OH and SCOF-Br were prepared accordingly. It can be seen from Fig. S16 that the light absorption ranges of COF-OH (at \(606~\mathrm{nm}\) ) was broader than any other COFs in the present study, while the band gap of COF-OH was the narrowest among these COFs (Fig. S17). It turns out that the monomers skeleton or the substituting groups can effectively regulate chemical structure of the COFs and thus their band structure. Afterwards, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 was applied the core and added in the synthetic process of the above three COFs, and other three covalently connected core–shell MOFs@COFs hybrid materials, namely, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-Br, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@SCOF-OH, and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@SCOF-Br, were fabricated by the same strategy. Obviously, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH exhibited the widest light absorption range among these hybrid materials, and their band gap had an order of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@CO F-OH < \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@SCOF-OH < \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@SCOF-Br < \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -
MIL-125@COF-Br (Fig. 5a). That is to say, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH had the highest photocatalytic performance. The \(C O_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction performance of these materials was performed, and actually it is observed that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH exhibited the highest CO yield (Fig. 5(b, c)).
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the COF-OH was successfully anchored on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 by adding \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 into the synthetic process of COF-OH. A kind of novel core–shell \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH hybrid materials was covalently connected by the stable \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) bonds, which endowed the hybrid materials had higher physicochemical stability. And the \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) covalent bonds and II-Sc heme heterostructure at the interface layer acted as the bridges to accelerate the charge transfer and enhanced the separation efficiency of photogenerated electrons and holes. The \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@COF-OH-3 exhibited the highest CO yield of \(22.93\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.\) - \(\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) , about 2 times higher than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 \((11.82\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) and 3 times higher than COF-OH \((7.26\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) . The broader visible light absorption range and narrow band gap of COF-OH, and the matched band gap between the core and the shell afforded it suitable for the \(C O_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction. This work provides novel inspirations for the construction of the efficient and stable MOFs@COFs hybrid materials.
